--- body ---
## Context
The /roar sweep after X2 found two bench drivers pinned to the pre-rename
index schema (0100). Two instances of one assumption is a class, and the
class has a nasty shape: upstream owns the schema, this repo owns the
drivers, and nothing connects them. The rename happened upstream, every
driver here kept passing `ruff` and `pytest`, and the breakage surfaced only
when someone tried to run one — an hour into a measurement session, against
a deadline.

It will happen again. Upstream renames tables when it wants to, and this repo
has no notification, no version pin on the schema, and no test that opens an
index at all. `make check` covers the harness's own Python and the figure
guard; it never touches SQLite.

The guard must therefore fire on a change nobody thought to connect to
`bench/` — which is why it is a standing test rather than a line in 0100's
diff.

## Relevant files
- `tests/` — currently `test_check_figures.py` and `test_query_latency.py`,
  both pure-Python; nothing here opens a database
- `bench/index_concentration.mjs`, `bench/bm25_idf_effect.mjs`,
  `bench/constrained_match.mjs`, `bench/query.py` — the drivers that take a
  real index path
- `UPSTREAM` — carries the reviewed SHA, and is the natural place to record
  the schema generation the drivers expect

## Actions
1. Build a TINY current-schema fixture — a few passages, both tables,
   committed or generated by a script in seconds — not the 949 MB real index.
   The fixture is the positive control: without one, a guard that finds
   nothing is indistinguishable from a guard that could not look.
2. Add a test, `integration`-marked, that runs each real-index driver against
   the fixture and asserts it produces its artifact shape.
3. Add the negative case: an old-schema fixture, against which every driver
   must FAIL LOUDLY and name the schema it found. This is the case that would
   have gone red before 0100 and is the one that earns the suite's place.
4. Record in `UPSTREAM` which schema generation the drivers target, so a
   future upstream rename has somewhere to land.

## Test
The negative fixture IS the test — it must fail against the pre-0100 drivers.
Verify by checking out a driver at its current SHA and confirming the new
test goes red; a guard never seen to fail is not a guard.

## Verification
- [ ] the old-schema fixture makes the pre-0100 drivers go red
- [ ] the current-schema fixture makes the repaired drivers go green
- [ ] fixtures are small enough that `make check` stays under its budget
- [ ] `UPSTREAM` names the expected schema generation

## Invariants
- The fast tier stays pure Python: these tests are `integration`, per the
  harness's cost-based tiering.
- No test depends on the author's 949 MB index or on a live Zotero.

## Exit criteria
- A standing test opens each real-index driver against both fixtures, the
  negative case has been SEEN to fail against the old drivers, and `make
  check` is green.
